XXIII. 🔱 R6.22 CANONICAL INVARIANTS

R6.22-I01
Historical constitutional states SHALL remain distinguishable from current state.

R6.22-I02
Stored history SHALL NOT automatically be considered proven memory.

R6.22-I03
Historical reconstruction SHALL be independently reproducible.

R6.22-I04
Historical replay SHALL use the rulesets applicable to the reconstructed period.

R6.22-I05
Current rulesets SHALL NOT retroactively define historical states.

R6.22-I06
Memory gaps SHALL remain explicitly represented.

R6.22-I07
AI-generated reconstruction candidates SHALL NOT constitute historical proof.

R6.22-I08
TRIAS SHALL verify reconstruction without fabricating missing history.

R6.22-I09
FOOL SHALL be able to challenge historical reconstruction.

R6.22-I10
Archived history SHALL remain preserved where retention permits.

R6.22-I11
Archived constitutional states SHALL remain independently verifiable.

R6.22-I12
Corrections SHALL preserve the reconstructability of the pre-correction state.

R6.22-I13
Federated histories SHALL remain independently reconstructible.

R6.22-I14
Migration history SHALL remain reconstructible across lineage transitions.

R6.22-I15
Historical validity SHALL NOT depend on present ACTIVE status.

R6.22-I16
A reconstruction SHALL reproduce the target watermark or explicitly report divergence.

R6.22-I17
Loss of required historical artifacts SHALL produce an explicit retention or memory gap.

R6.22-I18
The system SHALL NOT claim historical reconstruction beyond the available proof boundary.


