MEMORY_CORRUPTED
MEMORY_UNRECOVERABLE


---

XIII. 🕰️ CRYPTOGRAPHIC OBSOLESCENCE

There is another important future problem.

A Watermark might remain historically valid while the cryptographic algorithm used to authenticate it becomes obsolete.

R6.23 therefore distinguishes:

HISTORICAL VALIDITY

from:

CURRENT CRYPTOGRAPHIC ACCEPTABILITY

A historical signature may be preserved as evidence even if the algorithm is no longer approved for new operations.

The system must not rewrite:

old signature

simply because:

new algorithm

exists.

Instead:

OLD SIGNATURE
      ↓
HISTORICAL ATTESTATION
      ↓
NEW ARCHIVAL ATTESTATION

The new attestation certifies preservation.

It does not pretend to be the original signature.


---

XIV. 🔄 CRYPTOGRAPHIC MIGRATION

Therefore:

W₁
 │
 ├── historical signature
 │
 ▼
CRYPTOGRAPHIC RE-ATTESTATION
 │
 ▼
ARCHIVE ATTESTATION A₁

This is analogous to R6.20 lineage migration.

The cryptographic representation can evolve while historical identity remains preserved.

Thus:

RE-ATTESTATION SHALL NOT REWRITE HISTORICAL AUTHENTICATION.


---

XV. 🧬 SCHEMA OBSOLESCENCE

The same applies to data schemas.

Suppose:

Schema v1

becomes:

Schema v7

A historical record encoded in v1 must not simply be rewritten as v7 and presented as though it originally existed in v7.

Instead:

Historical Schema v1
        ↓
Schema Translation
        ↓
Current Representation v7

The original representation remains preserved where required.

And:

TRANSLATION_PROOF

demonstrates that the transformation is deterministic and loss characteristics are understood.


---

XVI. 🚨 LOSS CLASSIFICATION

Not all historical loss is identical.

R6.23 introduces explicit classification:

FULLY_RECOVERABLE
PARTIALLY_RECOVERABLE
INTEGRITY_UNCERTAIN
PROVENANCE_LOST
RECONSTRUCTION_BLOCKED
IRRECOVERABLE

This prevents the dangerous binary:

exists / doesn't exist

from hiding degraded historical knowledge.


---

XVII. 🏛️ CITADEL RETIREMENT

A Citadel may eventually retire:

ACTIVE
 ↓
RETIRING
 ↓
ARCHIVED
 ↓
OFFLINE

But its constitutional memory must survive the operational lifetime of the Citadel.

Therefore:

CITADEL RETIREMENT
        ↓
FINAL WATERMARK
        ↓
MEMORY MANIFEST
        ↓
ARCHIVAL ATTESTATION
        ↓
INDEPENDENT STORAGE

The Citadel can disappear operationally.

Its constitutional history cannot disappear merely because its runtime did.


---

XVIII. 🌌 MEMORY HANDOFF

When responsibility for archival preservation moves:

ARCHIVE A
   ↓
TRANSFER
   ↓
ARCHIVE B

the transfer itself becomes a constitutional event.

MEMORY_TRANSFER_PROOF

must establish:

source memory root
target memory root
artifact manifest
transfer event
integrity verification
receiving attestation

Therefore:

MEMORY TRANSFER MUST ITSELF BE PART OF MEMORY.

Beautifully recursive.


