XXII. 🔥 R6.23 CANONICAL INVARIANTS

R6.23-I01
Historical memory SHALL NOT depend on a single storage location.

R6.23-I02
Every trusted archive SHALL possess a verifiable memory root.

R6.23-I03
Historical artifacts SHALL be integrity-addressable.

R6.23-I04
Archive manifests SHALL identify the material required for reconstruction.

R6.23-I05
Backup availability SHALL NOT constitute historical proof by itself.

R6.23-I06
Recovered history SHALL be independently replayable.

R6.23-I07
Recovery divergence SHALL be explicitly recorded.

R6.23-I08
Replica agreement SHALL NOT substitute for constitutional proof.

R6.23-I09
AI analysis SHALL NOT independently adjudicate archival conflicts.

R6.23-I10
TRIAS SHALL verify archival integrity without fabricating missing artifacts.

R6.23-I11
FOOL SHALL be able to challenge memory integrity and recovery claims.

R6.23-I12
Historical signatures SHALL NOT be rewritten merely because cryptographic standards evolve.

R6.23-I13
Cryptographic re-attestation SHALL preserve the distinction between original and contemporary attestations.

R6.23-I14
Historical schemas SHALL remain distinguishable from translated current schemas.

R6.23-I15
Schema migration SHALL be accompanied by a verifiable transformation record.

R6.23-I16
Citadel retirement SHALL NOT imply constitutional memory retirement.

R6.23-I17
Memory transfers SHALL themselves be provenance-bound events.

R6.23-I18
Memory degradation SHALL remain explicitly represented.

R6.23-I19
Archived memory SHALL remain independently verifiable where technically possible.

R6.23-I20
No system SHALL claim complete historical memory beyond its demonstrated preservation boundary.


