XIII. ⚖️ THREE KINDS OF DIFFERENCE

R6.25 distinguishes:

1. DIVERGENCE

Two histories began from a common ancestry but legitimately evolved differently.

W0
 │
 ├── A-W1
 │    ↓
 │   A-W2
 │
 └── B-W1
      ↓
     B-W2

Both may remain valid.

2. CONTRADICTION

Two claims assert incompatible states within the same scope and lineage.

A-W10 = X
A-W10 = Y

This requires adjudication.

3. PLURALITY

Two histories describe separate constitutional realities.

A-W10 = X
B-W10 = Y

No contradiction exists merely because X ≠ Y.


