☄️🌱 THE MACHINE KNOWS WHAT IT KNOWS.

💧 THE MACHINE KNOWS WHAT IT CANNOT YET PROVE.

🦆 THE MACHINE PERMITS SOMEONE ELSE TO QUESTION BOTH.

🔬 AND THE RECORD PRESERVES THE DIFFERENCE FOREVER.

🔒 R6.28 — CANONICAL ENGINEERING BUILD SEALED

Watermark anchors the past.
Lineage preserves ancestry.
Graph preserves relationships.
Evidence supports claims.
Proof establishes knowledge.
The Boundary prevents overclaiming.

PROOF IS NOT THE ABSENCE OF DOUBT.

PROOF IS THE PRESENCE OF A CLOSED, REPRODUCIBLE, CHALLENGEABLE JUSTIFICATION.

☄️🌱 R6.29 — CONSTITUTIONAL PROOF REVOCATION & EPISTEMIC REGENERATION PROTOCOL

PROOF MAY AGE — HISTORY MAY NOT BE ERASED

Status: 🔒 CANONICAL ENGINEERING BUILD
Parent: R6.28 — Constitutional Knowledge Boundary & Proof Closure Protocol
Core primitive: PROOF_STATE
Secondary primitives: REVOCATION_EVENT, REVALIDATION_EVENT, EPISTEMIC_GENERATION, PROOF_DEPENDENCY

Primary invariant

> A constitutional proof state SHALL remain historically immutable, but its present validity MAY be changed only through a new evidence-bearing, provenance-bound, independently verifiable transition.



R6.28 established the boundary of knowledge.

R6.29 addresses what happens when that boundary changes.

Because eventually the machine encounters:

PROVEN
  ↓
NEW EVIDENCE
  ↓
?

The answer may be:

REVALIDATED

but it may also be:

CONTESTED

or:

REVOKED

And PALACO must distinguish these states without rewriting history.


---

I. 💧 PROOF IS A HISTORICAL OBJECT

A proof is not simply:

proof = true

It is:

PROOF
 ├── CLAIM
 ├── EVIDENCE
 ├── PROVENANCE
 ├── RULESET
 ├── VERIFICATION
 ├── REPLAY
 ├── CLOSURE
 └── WATERMARK

Once closed, that historical proof record remains immutable.

Therefore:

OLD_PROOF
   ↓
NEW_EVIDENCE

does not modify OLD_PROOF.

Instead:

OLD_PROOF
   ↓
REVOCATION / REVALIDATION EVENT
   ↓
NEW_PROOF_STATE


---

II. 🔱 PROOF STATE

Conceptually:

pub enum ProofState {
    Open,
    Proven,
    Contested,
    Suspended,
    Revoked,
    Revalidated,
    Irrecoverable,
}

These states describe the current epistemic status.

The historical transitions remain separately preserved.


---

III. 🧬 PROOF GENERATIONS

A proof may have generations:

P₀
 │
 └── Evidence E₀
      ↓
    VERIFIED
      ↓
    CLOSED
      │
      ▼
    P₁
      │
   New Evidence
      ↓
    REVIEW
      ↓
    REVALIDATED

Thus:

ProofGeneration = 0
ProofGeneration = 1
ProofGeneration = 2
...

Each generation is independently traceable.

NEW KNOWLEDGE CREATES A NEW PROOF STATE — NOT A NEW PAST.


---

IV. 🚨 REVOCATION

Suppose:

P17 = PROVEN

Later:

E92

demonstrates that a mandatory premise was false.

PALACO records:

P17
 ↓
REVOCATION_EVENT
 ↓
P18 = REVOKED

Not:

P17 = NEVER_PROVEN

That would falsify the historical record.

The correct statement is:

> P17 was historically proven under the evidence and rules available at the time, and was subsequently revoked after new evidence established a material defect.



That distinction is extraordinarily important for forensic integrity.


---

V. 🕰️ HISTORICAL VALIDITY VS PRESENT VALIDITY

A proof therefore has two dimensions:

HISTORICAL_STATUS

and:

CURRENT_STATUS

Example:

Historical:
PROVEN

Current:
REVOKED

There is no contradiction.

It means:

THE PROOF EXISTED

and:

THE PROOF NO LONGER JUSTIFIES PRESENT AUTHORITY


---

VI. 🌳 DEPENDENCY REVOCATION

A deeper problem appears when:

P1
 ↓
P2
 ↓
P3
 ↓
ACTION

and later:

P1 → REVOKED

Does P2 remain valid?

PALACO must not guess.

It constructs the dependency graph:

P1
 │
 ▼
P2
 │
 ▼
P3
 │
 ▼
ACTION

and determines which downstream claims depend materially upon the revoked proof.

Possible result:

P1 → REVOKED
P2 → IMPACTED
P3 → IMPACTED
ACTION → REVIEW_REQUIRED

This becomes:

PROOF DEPENDENCY ANALYSIS


---

VII. 🧠 BRAINPOWER DISCOVERS IMPACT

BRAINPOWER can calculate:

dependency paths
affected claims
downstream actions
potentially invalidated decisions
related watermarks
related Citadel states

But again:

BRAINPOWER
   ↓
IMPACT ANALYSIS

does not equal:

BRAINPOWER
   ↓
AUTOMATIC REVOCATION

The epistemic transition requires the appropriate constitutional verification.


---

VIII. 🔬 TRIAS REVALIDATION

TRIAS determines whether the new evidence actually affects the proof.

NEW EVIDENCE
     ↓
DEPENDENCY ANALYSIS
     ↓
TRIAS REVIEW
     ↓
┌───────────────┬───────────────┬──────────────┐
▼               ▼               ▼
VALID          CONTESTED       REVOKED

Or:

NEW_EVIDENCE
     ↓
NO MATERIAL IMPACT
     ↓
REVALIDATED

The old proof remains part of history in every case.


---

IX. 🦆 FOOL AS PROOF-REVOCATION TRIGGER

FOOL may challenge an existing proof:

PROOF_CHALLENGE
      ↓
NEW_EVIDENCE
      ↓
REVIEW

Possible grounds:

INVALID_EVIDENCE
BROKEN_PROVENANCE
RULESET_ERROR
SCOPE_OVERREACH
REPLAY_DIVERGENCE
AUTHORITY_ERROR
TEMPORAL_ERROR
DEPENDENCY_FAILURE
FABRICATED_CONTINUITY

FOOL does not revoke the proof directly.

FOOL creates the constitutional pressure for reassessment.


---

X. ⚓ QUAY PRESERVES THE ENTIRE PROOF LIFE

The Quay must preserve:

P17 CREATED
P17 VERIFIED
P17 CLOSED
P17 CHALLENGED
P17 REVIEWED
P17 REVOKED
P18 CREATED

Nothing is silently replaced.

Thus the forensic sequence remains:

P17
 ↓
CHALLENGE
 ↓
REVIEW
 ↓
REVOCATION
 ↓
P18

This creates an epistemic audit trail.


---

XI. 💀 NO RETROACTIVE PURIFICATION

PALACO must resist an extremely tempting operation:

"That proof was later shown to be wrong,
therefore it should disappear from history."

No.

The machine must preserve:

